{{$NEXT}}
  - Docker: one image, raudssus/mcp-wiki on Docker Hub, built and pushed by
    the dzil bundle at release; the second, separate build-and-push script
    and the local-only registry setting are gone
  - Docker: the entrypoint starts mcp-wiki without a shell in between, so a
    wiki path or any argument containing spaces or quotes arrives intact; it
    runs as the owner of the mounted wiki
  - Docker: the image no longer pins PERL5LIB to one perl version, and
    building it no longer tries to build an image from inside the image
  - bin/mcp-wiki starts with "#!/usr/bin/env perl"; installs rewrite it to the
    installing perl as before
  - restore_section writes the restored page: the document that the history
    returns replaces the page file instead of being pushed into a single
    section, which used to leave the whole page inside one heading.
  - Breaking: a section's text is its body without the heading line, in
    get_paragraph and update_paragraph alike. update_paragraph replaces the
    body only and leaves the heading and the following section untouched
    (it used to swallow the first line of the next section); content that
    still starts with the heading line now gets that heading twice. Writing
    back what get_paragraph returned leaves the page byte for byte as it was.
  - Breaking: heading_path is now the chain of a section's ancestors only.
    Siblings no longer leak into it: "# Intro", "## Alpha", "## Beta" yield
    Intro#Beta (was Intro#Alpha#Beta), and a following "# Other" yields
    Other (was Intro#Other). A section's address no longer changes when a
    sibling section is inserted before it. Addresses stored from earlier
    versions for any section after a sibling must be fetched anew via get_toc.
  - MCP::Wiki::Git stays as a deprecated thin wrapper around
    MCP::Wiki::Git::History
  - Removed unused internals: Document update_content_hash, Git::History
    history_file / _build_history_file (.mcp-wiki-history.json was never read
    or written), and the unused Digest::SHA, to_json and Types::Standard
    imports; Digest::SHA and Types::Standard leave the cpanfile
  - Docs: README tool table lists the real arguments of every tool
    (heading_path, not heading) and the known heading_path/paragraph/restore
    defects; mcp-wiki POD states what --git and --commit-reason-required do;
    PLAN.md is marked as the original draft; t/06-readme-tools.t keeps README
    and bin/mcp-wiki POD in sync with the tool schemas and Getopt options
  - Security: every tool is confined to the wiki root given at start
    (wiki_root / --wiki-root). Tools take no root_dir argument; page names are
    resolved (symlinks, "..") and must lie below the root.
  - Security: a page is a .md file. Tools refuse any other file and everything
    below a .git directory, so a wiki's repository is out of reach.
  - list_pages returns exactly the pages the other tools accept and does not
    enter .git
  - create_page and rename_page create missing subdirectories
  - cpanfile matches the code: Types::Standard is required, Git::Raw is
    recommended (history degrades cleanly without it), unused Markdown::Perl
    and Syntax::Keyword::Try are dropped; t/05-cpanfile.t keeps it in sync

0.001  2026-04-28
  - Initial release
  - MCP server for markdown wiki management
  - TOC extraction and paragraph-level editing
  - Git integration for history tracking